<h1>Tattoo Guides &amp; Resources</h1>
<p class="muted">Free, practical guides written from real shop experience — for people getting tattooed, artists who draw, and shops that tattoo.</p>

<p>Most tattoo websites are just galleries. We think the gallery is only half the job. Choosing a design is a permanent decision, healing one takes real care, and commissioning custom artwork goes much better when you know exactly what happens after you order. The guides below collect what our artists and partner shops explain to clients every day — how to pick artwork that ages well, how a custom design is drawn from brief to finished files, how to heal new ink without wrecking it, and how tattoo shops use Tattoo Art Customs to run bookings, payments, and storefronts. Everything here is free whether you buy from us or not.</p>

<section class="card">
  <h2><a href="/guides/aftercare">The Complete Tattoo Aftercare Guide</a></h2>
  <p>Healing decides how your tattoo looks for the rest of its life. This guide walks the whole timeline — the first wash, the peel and itch phase, when to moisturize and when to leave it alone, swimming, sun, and exercise rules, plus the warning signs that mean you should call a doctor instead of waiting it out. It also explains the two main healing methods, traditional wrap and second-skin film, and how to tell which one your artist used.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="/guides/aftercare">Read the aftercare guide</a></p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2><a href="/guides/choosing-a-design">How to Choose a Tattoo Design That Ages Well</a></h2>
  <p>A design that looks sharp on paper can blur into a blob in ten years if it is drawn wrong. This guide covers placement and body flow, why size and detail level matter more than people think, the major tattoo styles and how each one ages, how to judge linework before it goes on your skin, and the questions worth asking any artist before the needle starts. It also explains how design licenses work, so you know exactly what you are buying.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="/guides/choosing-a-design">Read the choosing guide</a></p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2><a href="/guides/first-tattoo">Your First Tattoo: A Complete, Calm Walkthrough</a></h2>
  <p>Your first tattoo is three projects wearing one coat: the decision, the transaction, and the healing. This walkthrough goes in the order it actually happens — the health and eligibility checks that come before everything, sitting with an idea until it proves it is not a mood, choosing an artist by healed photographs instead of fresh ones, the consultation and the honest price (deposit, hourly vs. piece, tipping), what to do the day of the appointment, and what the chair is really like, stencil to first line.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="/guides/first-tattoo">Read the first-tattoo walkthrough</a></p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2><a href="/guides/stencil-day">Stencil Day: Size, Placement &amp; the Mirror Check</a></h2>
  <p>Before any needle, the design is printed at true size and mapped onto your body — and that handoff is where size, placement, and orientation actually get decided. This guide covers the worked minimums (how tall script must stand to stay readable, why portraits under four inches lose the person), how designs distort in motion at wrists, elbows, and ribs, who a design should read correctly to, the symmetry tax on centered work, and the ten-minute mirror check that catches wrong size, wrong spot, and misspelled names while they are still free to fix.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="/guides/stencil-day">Read the stencil-day guide</a></p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2><a href="/guides/custom-design-process">How Custom Tattoo Designs Work, Step by Step</a></h2>
  <p>Ordering artwork from an artist you have never met feels risky until you can see the process. This guide lays out the whole path: how to write a brief that gets you the drawing you actually want, the deposit and timeline, what files you receive and in what form, how the design license works, and how to hand the artwork to any tattoo artist — here on the site or at your local shop — so it tattoos cleanly.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="/guides/custom-design-process">Read the custom process guide</a></p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2><a href="/guides/for-tattoo-shops">For Tattoo Shops: Run Your Business on Tattoo Art Customs</a></h2>
  <p>Shops on Tattoo Art Customs get more than a listing. This page explains the shop toolkit in plain language: your verified storefront, online booking and client profiles, tap-to-pay in person with no card reader, selling flash and premade designs, referral earnings on verified sales, and the back-office tools for inventory and expenses — plus the membership, what it costs, and how to join.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="/guides/for-tattoo-shops">Read the shops guide</a></p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2><a href="/styles">Tattoo Style Guides</a></h2>
  <p>Before the subject, pick the style — it decides how your tattoo behaves for the rest of its life. Our style guides cover every major style in the gallery, one complete page each: what the style is and where it comes from, how its designs are drawn, the placements that flatter it, the sizes it honestly needs, and how it ages. American traditional, neo-traditional, Japanese irezumi, blackwork, fine line, realism, watercolor, trash polka and more — including the parts some shops would rather not say out loud, like which styles fade fastest and which ones ask for touch-ups.</p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="/styles">Browse the style guides</a></p>
</section>


